  "client_view_text": " We assume a flush policy that ensures that every committed transaction is safely stored on disk before another transaction can proceed.\n\n## Step 1: Understanding the Write-Ahead Log\nA write-ahead log (WAL) is a component of a database's storage system that ensures the integrity of database transactions even in the event of a system crash or failure. It works by keeping a record of all changes made to the database before they are actually written to the database files on disk. This includes the changes made by each transaction.\n\n## Step 2: Transaction Commit Process\nWhen a transaction is committed, the database system records the transaction's changes in the We're talking about a transactional database, one that supports atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability.\n\nA write-ahead log (WAL), also known as a redo log, is a file that a database maintains to ensure that all transactions are properly logged before they are written to the database's main storage. When a database experiences a crash or failure, the WAL file can be used to recover the database to a consistent state.\n\nHere's how it works: each time a transaction is committed, the database writes a record to the WAL file that describes the changes made by the transaction. The record includes information such as the transaction ID, the data affected, and the type of change (e.g., insert, update, or delete). If the database crashes before it has a chance to write the changes to the main storage, the WAL file remains intact. When the database restarts, it reads the WAL file from the beginning and applies the changes described in each record to the main storage. This process is known as redoing the log.\n\nThe WAL file ensures that the database's main storage is consistent, meaning that all transactions are either fully committed or fully rolled back, without leaving any intermediate state. By redoing the log, the database can recover to a point in time"
}